1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
A refund can complete while the merchant's books still expect the original payment.
SettlementProof turns that difference into a specific case with inspectable payment evidence.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:32,000
This is an actual PayPal sandbox order, using fictitious funds.
The app retrieves the order, capture, and refund through authenticated API reads.
The merchant record independently expects one hundred dollars and no refund.

3
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:52,000
PayPal shows a hundred-dollar capture and a completed twenty-five-dollar refund.
Observed net is seventy-five dollars, before fees.
The missing refund creates a twenty-five-dollar difference.
Every amount links to its capture or refund resource.

4
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Ask for unbooked refunds. A locally trained intent classifier selects the refund investigation.
The ledger supplies the facts, and the interface exposes the model's supporting words.
It proposes a review step without changing payments or the books.

5
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Now record that refund in the merchant expectation.
The same PayPal evidence reconciles: seventy-five expected, seventy-five observed, zero difference.
This is a controlled comparison using the same authenticated sandbox resources.

6
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Switching to clearly labeled synthetic fixtures lets us test a different failure: repeated delivery.
This webhook appears twice, but the capture still counts once.
Accounting follows the payment resource, not the number of notifications.
The evidence identifies the ignored repeat.

7
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:10,000
The classifier has a narrow job. An unrelated request produces a clarification.
It is not a financial advisor or a generative agent,
and its model support is not a calibrated confidence score.

8
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Twenty-five tests check the core invariants. The demo runs locally without a paid model API.
SettlementProof: payment differences, explained with evidence.
